1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,641
Imagine you're floating in the middle of the Pacific

2
00:00:03,641 --> 00:00:06,652
Ocean. The water is warm, the sky is clear.

3
00:00:06,652 --> 00:00:10,573
Now imagine something pulls you straight down. Below you

4
00:00:10,573 --> 00:00:13,584
is the deepest place on our planet. How far

5
00:00:13,584 --> 00:00:16,945
would you get before the ocean stopped you? Stay

6
00:00:16,945 --> 00:00:20,305
to the end, because what scientists found at the

7
00:00:20,305 --> 00:00:22,336
bottom is the strangest part.

8
00:00:22,886 --> 00:00:26,848
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the strangest

9
00:00:26,848 --> 00:00:30,810
places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we sink

10
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:34,150
nearly seven miles into the Mariana Trench.

11
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,458
At its very bottom is a spot called the

12
00:00:37,458 --> 00:00:41,773
Challenger Deep, almost thirty six thousand feet down. That's

13
00:00:41,773 --> 00:00:44,956
nearly seven miles of water stacked on top of

14
00:00:44,956 --> 00:00:45,239
you.

15
00:00:45,789 --> 00:00:49,897
Thousands of people have climbed Mount Everest. But fewer

16
00:00:49,897 --> 00:00:53,573
than thirty have ever reached this spot, all inside

17
00:00:53,573 --> 00:00:56,889
machines built to survive it. You would not be

18
00:00:56,889 --> 00:00:59,700
in a machine. So let's go down, step by

19
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:00,061
step.

20
00:01:00,611 --> 00:01:03,957
First, the scale. The average depth of the ocean

21
00:01:03,957 --> 00:01:07,513
is about two point three miles. The Challenger Deep

22
00:01:07,513 --> 00:01:10,232
is nearly three times deeper than that.

23
00:01:10,782 --> 00:01:14,253
If you dropped Mount Everest, the tallest mountain on

24
00:01:14,253 --> 00:01:17,332
Earth, into the trench, its peak would still be

25
00:01:17,332 --> 00:01:19,166
more than a mile underwater.

26
00:01:19,716 --> 00:01:22,445
The trench itself is a giant scar in the

27
00:01:22,445 --> 00:01:26,608
seafloor, curving for more than fifteen hundred miles through

28
00:01:26,608 --> 00:01:29,679
the western Pacific, near the island of Guam.

29
00:01:30,229 --> 00:01:33,771
It formed because one giant piece of Earth's crust,

30
00:01:33,771 --> 00:01:38,078
the Pacific Plate, is slowly sliding underneath another. Where

31
00:01:38,078 --> 00:01:41,620
they meet, the seafloor bends down into the planet.

32
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:44,607
And you are falling straight into that gap.

33
00:01:45,157 --> 00:01:48,101
How do we even know it's this deep? In

34
00:01:48,101 --> 00:01:51,897
1875, the crew of a British ship, HMS Challenger,

35
00:01:51,897 --> 00:01:55,538
lowered a weighted rope over the side here, and

36
00:01:55,538 --> 00:01:59,257
it just kept going down. Much later, ships using

37
00:01:59,257 --> 00:02:03,595
sound waves bouncing off the seafloor mapped the deepest

38
00:02:03,595 --> 00:02:06,384
point, and named it after that ship.

39
00:02:06,934 --> 00:02:10,092
Is there anywhere deeper? Not that we know of.

40
00:02:10,092 --> 00:02:13,113
The runner up, the Tonga Trench in the South

41
00:02:13,113 --> 00:02:16,957
Pacific, comes close, but the Mariana Trench still holds

42
00:02:16,957 --> 00:02:19,841
the record for the deepest point on Earth.

43
00:02:20,391 --> 00:02:23,549
The first thing you'd notice is the light. At

44
00:02:23,549 --> 00:02:27,058
about six hundred fifty feet, most of the sunlight

45
00:02:27,058 --> 00:02:30,216
is already gone. The water turns a deep, dark

46
00:02:30,216 --> 00:02:30,567
blue.

47
00:02:31,117 --> 00:02:34,774
By around three thousand feet, there is no sunlight

48
00:02:34,774 --> 00:02:37,571
at all. Not dim. None. From here to the

49
00:02:37,571 --> 00:02:40,942
bottom, it is darker than the darkest night you

50
00:02:40,942 --> 00:02:42,018
have ever seen.

51
00:02:42,568 --> 00:02:46,998
Scientists call the deepest zone, below about twenty thousand

52
00:02:46,998 --> 00:02:50,557
feet, the hadal zone. It's named after Hades, the

53
00:02:50,557 --> 00:02:53,970
Greek god of the underworld. And you still have

54
00:02:53,970 --> 00:02:55,859
more than six miles to go.

55
00:02:56,409 --> 00:02:59,307
Quick question before we go deeper. How deep do

56
00:02:59,307 --> 00:03:02,267
you think you could dive before you'd turn back?

57
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:03,747
Tell me in the comments.

58
00:03:04,297 --> 00:03:07,295
As the light fades, so does the heat. The

59
00:03:07,295 --> 00:03:10,659
warm surface water is only a thin layer. Below

60
00:03:10,659 --> 00:03:13,364
it, the ocean gets colder and colder.

61
00:03:13,914 --> 00:03:16,777
At the bottom of the trench, the water sits

62
00:03:16,777 --> 00:03:20,838
between about thirty four and thirty nine degrees Fahrenheit.

63
00:03:20,838 --> 00:03:22,170
Just above freezing.

64
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:26,501
In water that cold, an unprotected human loses heat

65
00:03:26,501 --> 00:03:30,727
incredibly fast. Your body would start shutting down long

66
00:03:30,727 --> 00:03:34,434
before the bottom. But honestly, cold would be the

67
00:03:34,434 --> 00:03:36,139
least of your problems.

68
00:03:36,689 --> 00:03:40,723
Here's what really makes the Mariana Trench deadly. Pressure.

69
00:03:40,723 --> 00:03:43,633
Right now, the air above you presses on your

70
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:47,072
body with about fifteen pounds on every square inch.

71
00:03:47,072 --> 00:03:50,180
You don't feel it, because you've lived with it

72
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:51,238
your whole life.

73
00:03:51,788 --> 00:03:54,967
In the ocean, every thirty three feet of water

74
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:58,354
adds that same amount again. Go down thirty three

75
00:03:58,354 --> 00:04:02,016
feet, and the pressure doubles. Go down three hundred

76
00:04:02,016 --> 00:04:05,057
thirty feet, and it's eleven times stronger.

77
00:04:05,607 --> 00:04:08,902
At the bottom of the Challenger Deep, the pressure

78
00:04:08,902 --> 00:04:11,868
is more than one thousand times what you feel

79
00:04:11,868 --> 00:04:15,427
at the surface. About sixteen thousand pounds on every

80
00:04:15,427 --> 00:04:18,919
square inch. Like an elephant standing on your thumb.

81
00:04:19,469 --> 00:04:22,098
So what would that do to you? The parts

82
00:04:22,098 --> 00:04:25,063
of your body filled with air would go first.

83
00:04:25,063 --> 00:04:28,501
Your lungs, your sinuses, your ears. The air inside

84
00:04:28,501 --> 00:04:32,140
them gets squeezed smaller and smaller, and your lungs

85
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:34,701
would collapse long before the bottom.

86
00:04:35,251 --> 00:04:38,695
The water in your body doesn't compress much, which

87
00:04:38,695 --> 00:04:41,531
is why deep sea animals can live here. But

88
00:04:41,531 --> 00:04:43,827
a body built for air simply can't.

89
00:04:44,377 --> 00:04:48,957
This pressure doesn't just crush people. It crushes machines

90
00:04:48,957 --> 00:04:52,850
too. In January 1960, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh

91
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:56,895
became the first humans to reach the Challenger Deep,

92
00:04:56,895 --> 00:04:59,567
inside a vessel called the Trieste.

93
00:05:00,117 --> 00:05:02,854
On the way down, they heard a loud crack.

94
00:05:02,854 --> 00:05:05,992
One of the outer window panes had cracked under

95
00:05:05,992 --> 00:05:09,530
the pressure. They kept going anyway, and spent about

96
00:05:09,530 --> 00:05:11,466
twenty minutes at the bottom.

97
00:05:12,016 --> 00:05:15,610
Nobody went back for fifty two years. Then in

98
00:05:15,610 --> 00:05:19,922
2012, filmmaker James Cameron made the first solo dive

99
00:05:19,922 --> 00:05:21,040
to the bottom.

100
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:25,742
And starting in 2019, explorer Victor Vescovo began a

101
00:05:25,742 --> 00:05:29,424
series of dives in a submersible built for full

102
00:05:29,424 --> 00:05:33,890
ocean depth, bringing others down with him, including the

103
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:35,222
son of Don Walsh.

104
00:05:35,772 --> 00:05:38,599
So here's my question for you. If you got

105
00:05:38,599 --> 00:05:41,633
a free seat in that submarine, would you go?

106
00:05:41,633 --> 00:05:43,495
Yes or no, in the comments.

107
00:05:44,045 --> 00:05:47,187
So the bottom of the trench is dark, freezing,

108
00:05:47,187 --> 00:05:50,466
and crushing. Surely nothing lives there. Wrong.

109
00:05:51,016 --> 00:05:55,793
Small shrimp-like creatures called amphipods scuttle across the floor.

110
00:05:55,793 --> 00:05:59,137
Some are far bigger than their cousins in shallow

111
00:05:59,137 --> 00:06:02,617
water, and they swarm anything that sinks down from

112
00:06:02,617 --> 00:06:03,027
above.

113
00:06:03,577 --> 00:06:08,511
There are xenophyophores, giant single-celled living things that can

114
00:06:08,511 --> 00:06:11,559
grow as big as your fist. One single cell,

115
00:06:11,559 --> 00:06:13,155
the size of your fist.

116
00:06:13,705 --> 00:06:17,219
And a little higher up live snailfish. Small, soft,

117
00:06:17,219 --> 00:06:21,008
almost see-through fish that live deeper than any other

118
00:06:21,008 --> 00:06:22,111
fish we know of.

119
00:06:22,661 --> 00:06:26,501
All of them survive on scraps. Dead plankton, fish,

120
00:06:26,501 --> 00:06:30,416
and the occasional dead whale that slowly sinks down

121
00:06:30,416 --> 00:06:34,407
from the sunlit world miles above. Down here, nothing

122
00:06:34,407 --> 00:06:35,461
goes to waste.

123
00:06:36,011 --> 00:06:39,258
But not everything at the bottom is alive. On

124
00:06:39,258 --> 00:06:44,380
several expeditions, explorers spotted something they never expected at

125
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:47,339
the deepest place on Earth. Plastic bags.

126
00:06:47,889 --> 00:06:51,470
Think about that. Humans have barely been there. But

127
00:06:51,470 --> 00:06:54,363
our trash got there first. No place on our

128
00:06:54,363 --> 00:06:56,635
planet is truly beyond our reach.

129
00:06:57,185 --> 00:06:59,958
So let's go back to you, sinking through the

130
00:06:59,958 --> 00:07:02,983
dark. The light would disappear in the first few

131
00:07:02,983 --> 00:07:06,386
minutes. The cold would start shutting your body down.

132
00:07:06,386 --> 00:07:09,222
And the pressure would squeeze the air out of

133
00:07:09,222 --> 00:07:10,924
you long before the bottom.

134
00:07:11,474 --> 00:07:14,757
The Mariana Trench isn't just deep. It's one of

135
00:07:14,757 --> 00:07:18,040
the most hostile places on our planet. And yet,

136
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:21,044
life found a way to live there, quietly, in

137
00:07:21,044 --> 00:07:24,886
total darkness. There may still be creatures down there

138
00:07:24,886 --> 00:07:26,493
no human has ever seen.

139
00:07:27,043 --> 00:07:30,713
Last question. Where should we go next, somewhere just

140
00:07:30,713 --> 00:07:33,364
as extreme, but on land? Tell me in the

141
00:07:33,364 --> 00:07:33,976
comments.

142
00:07:34,526 --> 00:07:38,323
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this

143
00:07:38,323 --> 00:07:42,049
journey, like the video, subscribe, and share it with

144
00:07:42,049 --> 00:07:45,072
someone who loves the ocean. See you on the

145
00:07:45,072 --> 00:07:45,705
next one.

